const faq = [
  { q: 'What sounds can ravens mimic?', a: 'Ravens are capable of mimicking a wide range of sounds: human words and phrases, other bird calls (including other raven calls they have not heard before), environmental sounds (machinery, water, creaking gates), and mammal calls. Captive ravens in close contact with humans often acquire extensive vocabularies of human speech. Wild ravens mimic other species\' alarm calls and have been documented using the calls of other animals (e.g., mammalian predators) in apparent deception contexts, though this interpretation is debated.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens understand what they are saying?', a: 'The evidence for semantic understanding (knowing what words mean) in ravens is limited. Some captive ravens use specific sounds in context-appropriate ways — producing a human word when shown the associated object — but this may reflect conditioned association rather than linguistic understanding. Ravens are not known to spontaneously combine words into novel sentences. Their mimicry, while impressive, appears primarily social and contextual rather than linguistic.' },
