    <p class="qa-pillar">Behaviour · BEH·12</p>
    <h1>Can Ravens Talk? Mimicry, Speech, and What Ravens Actually Do</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Yes — ravens can and do mimic human speech, often with striking clarity. Captive ravens in close contact with people regularly acquire words and short phrases. Wild ravens mimic other birds' calls, environmental sounds, and even some human sounds. What they do not appear to do is understand language in a semantic sense: their mimicry is impressive but is not the same as the speech-comprehension shown by some parrots.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Mimicry Is — and Is Not</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Vocal mimicry is the reproduction of sounds not in a species' innate repertoire — especially the sounds of other species. It is not the same as language or communication with meaning. A raven that says "Hello" has learned to produce that acoustic pattern; whether it associates any specific meaning with the sound is a separate and much harder question.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Ravens are "open-ended vocal learners" — they can acquire new sounds throughout their lives, not just in an early sensitive period. This is relatively rare among birds (parrots, corvids, hummingbirds, some shorebirds, and oscine songbirds are the main groups with this capacity) and is associated with complex social environments and large brains.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Mimic</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The range of sounds ravens mimic includes:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Human speech:</strong> captive ravens in contact with people acquire words, phrases, and sometimes longer fragments of human speech. The quality can be very close to the original — ravens can mimic human voices at the pitch and timbre of the individual they have heard, not just a generic human voice.</li>
      <li><strong>Other bird calls:</strong> ravens have been documented mimicking the calls of eagles and other raptors. Marzluff & Angell (2005) describe wild ravens producing raptor calls apparently to manipulate other ravens' behaviour — causing competitors to disperse while the mimic accesses food. This "deceptive mimicry" interpretation is intriguing but difficult to confirm definitively.</li>
      <li><strong>Environmental sounds:</strong> captive ravens have been recorded mimicking machinery, closing doors, running water, and other non-biological sounds from their environment.</li>
      <li><strong>Mammal calls:</strong> calls of wolves, dogs, and other mammals have been reported in raven mimicry accounts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Historical Accounts</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Accounts of talking ravens go back at least to classical antiquity. Pliny the Elder (<em>Natural History</em>, 77 AD) described a raven at Rome that greeted emperors by name, and noted that ravens could be taught to speak. Konrad Lorenz, who kept ravens in the twentieth century, described a raven that accurately reproduced his spoken name. Charles Dickens kept a pet raven named Grip who was reported to say several phrases; Grip inspired Edgar Allan Poe's poem "The Raven" (1845).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Evidence Says About Understanding</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The question of whether ravens understand what they say has not been resolved by controlled experiments. Some observations suggest context-appropriate use: a captive raven producing a food-related phrase specifically when fed, for instance. But the same behaviour can be explained by conditioning (the sound has been reinforced in that context) rather than semantic understanding.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>African Grey Parrots, in Irene Pepperberg's decades of research, showed stronger evidence of contextual word use — combining colour, shape, and number words in novel combinations. No equivalent series of experiments has been conducted on ravens. The gap in the evidence should not be read as evidence that ravens are less capable; it reflects where researchers have focussed, not necessarily a cognitive ceiling.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What We Don't Know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>How common is wild raven mimicry of human speech (as opposed to captive birds)?</li>
      <li>Is "deceptive" use of raptor calls or other species' calls genuinely intentional, or coincidental?</li>
      <li>Can ravens learn from hearing humans talk to each other, or only from direct interaction?</li>
      <li>Is there a maximum vocabulary size for ravens, and how does it compare to parrots?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

